# You normally don't need to edit this file directly -- run
# scripts/setup_venv_envs.sh or scripts/setup_conda_envs.sh instead, which
# creates .env for you and already fills in PANOPTIC_REPO_PATH and
# CELLMATE_PATH (it clones both repos into external/ automatically). The
# ONE thing neither script can do for you is below: PANOPTIC_MODEL_DIR,
# the fine-tuned model weights -- those are machine-specific, not in any
# repo. Add that, then run ./scripts/run_all.sh.
#
# Setting this up some other way instead? Every path below is
# machine-specific and none of it lives in this git repo, so see each
# comment for how to get it, then `cp .env.example .env` and edit by hand.

# ============================================================================
# Panoptic auto-segment model (panoptic_service/)
# ============================================================================

# The yeast Panoptic-DeepLab model repo (detectron2 code + the
# prediction.Predictor class panoptic_service/model_handler.py imports).
# This is a SEPARATE repo, not part of YeastPanopticWeb:
#   git clone https://github.com/wanlanli/PytrochDeepyeast.git /path/to/PytrochDeepyeast
PANOPTIC_REPO_PATH=/path/to/PytrochDeepyeast

# Where the fine-tuned checkpoint (model_final.pth) AND its own matching
# config.yaml live, saved together from the same training run. Copy this
# whole directory over (it's a few hundred MB-1GB) -- e.g.:
#   rsync -avP old-machine:/home/wlli/Data/oneformer_output/ /path/to/oneformer_output/
PANOPTIC_MODEL_DIR=/path/to/oneformer_output

# ============================================================================
# SAM point-prompt model (sam_service/)
# ============================================================================

# Either copy an existing checkpoint file here, or -- easier, since it's a
# public download -- leave this pointing at wherever you want it saved and run:
#   cd sam_service && python3 scripts/download_checkpoint.py vit_h
# (vit_b is much smaller/faster if this server has no GPU; must match
# SAM_MODEL_TYPE below.)
SAM_CHECKPOINT_PATH=/path/to/sam_vit_h_4b8939.pth
SAM_MODEL_TYPE=vit_h

# ============================================================================
# Quantification (CellMate library)
# ============================================================================

# A checkout of the CellMate quantification repo, with its Cython
# extensions already built for THIS machine's Python version (a prebuilt
# .so from another machine will NOT work if the Python version differs):
#   git clone https://github.com/wanlanli/CellMate.git /path/to/CellMate
#   cd /path/to/CellMate/cellmate/image_measure/measure
#   pip install Cython  # (into whichever venv you build with, e.g. backend/.venv)
#   python3 setup.py build_ext --inplace
CELLMATE_PATH=/path/to/CellMate

# ============================================================================
# Usually fine as-is -- all four processes run on this same machine
# ============================================================================
SAM_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:8100
PANOPTIC_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:8200

# Uncomment to force a device instead of auto-detecting (cuda if
# torch.cuda.is_available(), else cpu):
# SAM_DEVICE=cpu
# PANOPTIC_DEVICE=cpu

# ============================================================================
# How scripts/run_all.sh finds each service's Python (venv, the default, or
# conda -- e.g. if this machine has no sudo to install python3-venv, but
# does have conda already). See the comment at the top of that script for
# the exact one-time env-creation commands for each mode.
# ============================================================================
# PYTHON_ENV_MANAGER=conda

# Already have a conda/venv env with the right deps under a different name
# (e.g. an existing panoptic or SAM env you don't want to reinstall into a
# fresh yeastpanoptic-* one)? Point straight at its python3 instead -- this
# skips PYTHON_ENV_MANAGER's naming convention entirely, no renaming or
# reinstalling needed. Find the path with `conda env list` then
# `<that path>/bin/python3`, or `conda run -n <env> which python3`.
# BACKEND_PYTHON=/path/to/conda/envs/existing-backend-env/bin/python3
# SAM_SERVICE_PYTHON=/path/to/conda/envs/existing-sam-env/bin/python3
# PANOPTIC_SERVICE_PYTHON=/path/to/conda/envs/existing-panoptic-env/bin/python3
